\details{
Internal coded scale construction helpers

Developer documentation for the helpers that compute the coded combined
outcome contrast and the image of an unadjusted likelihood sublevel
set.

\code{delta_from_components()} computes
\code{p1 * (mu1 - atom) - p0 * (mu0 - atom)}. The Welch helper constructs the
coded observations directly and uses their arm specific sample means and
variances.

\code{delta_full_reference()} stores the arm samples, unrestricted arm means,
unrestricted arm logits, pooled residual sum of squares, and likelihood
constants for a successful unadjusted fit. \code{delta_full_candidate()}
evaluates the full criterion at \verb{(mu0, mu1, eta0, eta1)} and is also used to
verify the reduced endpoint calculations.

For the parametric likelihood, fix \verb{eta = (eta0, eta1)} and let \code{W_A} be the
sum of its two Bernoulli deviances. If the sublevel cutoff is \code{c}, the
remaining mean deviance is \code{q = c - W_A}. Write \code{M} for the total number of
nonatom observations and \code{SSE} for the unrestricted pooled residual sum of
squares. The corresponding weighted mean ellipsoid has squared radius
\code{Q = SSE * expm1(q / M)}. If \code{B} is the coded contrast at the fitted arm
means and
\code{H = sqrt(p0^2 / m0 + p1^2 / m1)}, its exact extrema over that ellipsoid are
\code{B - sqrt(Q) * H} and \code{B + sqrt(Q) * H}.

The remaining search is only over the compact Bernoulli sublevel set.
\code{delta_parametric_radial_limit()} writes
\verb{eta = eta_hat + r * (cos(phi), sin(phi))}. Strict convexity gives one
boundary radius on every ray. The radial helpers use a dense deterministic
angle and radius search, refine the best numerical extremum, reconstruct the attaining
means, and require \code{delta_full_candidate()} to equal the requested cutoff.

For the semiparametric likelihood, replace every atom by its numerical code
and retain each observed nonatom value. In one arm with \code{d} atoms, \code{m}
nonatoms, atom mass \code{rho}, nonatom mass \code{p}, and conditional nonatom weights
\code{q_j}, the Bernoulli likelihood ratio times the conditional empirical
likelihood ratio is exactly the ordinary empirical likelihood ratio for
the coded observations after setting each atom weight to \code{rho / d} and each
nonatom weight to \code{p * q_j}. Profiling subject to a coded mean therefore
gives the ordinary two sample coded outcome empirical likelihood exactly in
finite samples. \code{delta_splrt_sublevel_interval()} consequently inverts that
statistic at the supplied cutoff without numerical optimization over the
four component parameters.

\code{delta_sublevel_range()} dispatches to these two reductions. A projected
interval supplies a \code{chisq(df = 4)} cutoff.
}
